  <h2>Two jobs, two claims</h2>
  <p>When water or smoke damages a house, there are really two losses: the <strong>building</strong>, including walls, floors, cabinets and built-ins, and the <strong>contents</strong>, meaning everything you could pick up and take with you. Insurance treats them separately. The building is usually dwelling coverage; your belongings usually fall under personal property coverage, often labeled Coverage C, with its own limit and its own rules.</p>
  <p>Restoration companies often split them too. Many have a separate contents department, and some send contents work to a specialist company. That means a separate estimate, separate invoices and a separate set of decisions, often made fast, in the first day or two, while you're still dealing with the water.</p>

  <h2>Pack-out or move it within the house?</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Content manipulation</th><th>Pack-out</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>What happens</strong></td><td>Furniture and items are moved to a dry room or raised on blocks so drying can happen</td><td>Items are inventoried, packed and taken to the company's facility for cleaning and storage</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>When it fits</strong></td><td>One or two rooms of clean water, with dry space elsewhere in the house</td><td>Fire and smoke, large water losses, contaminated water, or rooms that must be empty for rebuilding</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>How it's billed</strong></td><td>Usually by the hour or by the room</td><td>Inventory, packing materials, labor, transport, cleaning by item and storage by the month</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Cost</strong></td><td>Modest</td><td>Often thousands of dollars, and storage keeps adding up</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>A pack-out is a real service and often the right call. It's also one of the more profitable parts of a restoration job, so it's sometimes suggested when moving things across the hall would do. You can accept, decline or limit it. Ask your adjuster what they'll approve before you agree.</p>

  <h2>How contents work is billed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Inventory:</strong> listing and photographing each item or box, often in specialized software.</li>
    <li><strong>Packing:</strong> labor plus boxes, wrap and crates, often per box.</li>
    <li><strong>Transport</strong> to and from the facility.</li>
    <li><strong>Cleaning,</strong> priced by item and method: wiping hard goods, ultrasonic cleaning for small hard items, laundering or dry cleaning textiles, ozone or other odor treatment after smoke.</li>
    <li><strong>Storage,</strong> usually by vault or by space, <strong>per month</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Pack-back:</strong> unpacking and placing items when you move back in.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="notice notice-amber"><strong>The meter that keeps running:</strong> storage bills every month until the rebuild finishes. If the rebuild drags on for permits, insurance disputes or contractor delays, storage can grow into one of the larger lines in the claim. Ask your adjuster early how long storage will be paid, and ask the company for monthly statements.</p>

  <h2>The inventory is your claim</h2>
  <p>For contents, insurers pay for what's documented. The inventory made during a pack-out, with photos, is often the backbone of your personal property claim. Two items on that list deserve the most attention:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Salvageable items</strong> are cleaned and returned. The claim covers the cleaning, not the item.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-salvageable items</strong> are discarded and claimed for their value. Each needs a description, an approximate age and what it would cost to replace.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Ask for a copy of the inventory as it's built, not just at the end. Add anything that's missing, such as items in closets, drawers or the garage, and correct descriptions that undersell what you owned. "Sofa" and "three-seat leather sofa, purchased 2021" lead to very different payments.</p>

  <h2>Nothing gets thrown away undocumented</h2>
  <p>This is where homeowners lose the most money. In the rush of the first day, wet carpet, soaked furniture and boxes of damaged belongings go to the dumpster. Without photos and a list, they're very hard to claim later.</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Photograph every item before it leaves,</strong> with any brand label or model number visible.</li>
    <li><strong>List it</strong> with a description, rough age and replacement cost.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep samples</strong> where you can: a piece of the carpet and pad, a corner of a damaged rug, fabric from upholstery.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask your adjuster</strong> whether they want to see anything before it's discarded, especially high-value items.</li>
    <li><strong>Sign off only on items you've seen listed.</strong> Ask for the disposal list in writing.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What can usually be saved</h2>
  <p>Whether something can be saved depends on the material, the <a href="/guides/water-damage-categories/">category of water</a>, and how long it was wet.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Clean water, caught quickly</th><th>Contaminated water</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Dishes, glass, metal, hard plastic</strong></td><td>Clean and keep</td><td>Usually cleanable with disinfection</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Clothing and linens</strong></td><td>Launder or dry clean</td><td>Often cleanable by specialist laundering</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Solid wood furniture</strong></td><td>Often restorable</td><td>Sometimes, depending on finish and exposure</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Upholstered furniture</strong></td><td>Sometimes, if dried quickly</td><td>Usually discarded</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Mattresses and box springs</strong></td><td>Sometimes, if only surface wet</td><td>Discarded</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Books, documents, photos</strong></td><td>Often saved by freezing, then freeze-drying</td><td>Specialist recovery for irreplaceable items</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Electronics</strong></td><td>Don't power on; specialists can test and clean</td><td>Specialist assessment</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Food, medicine, cosmetics</strong> that got wet</td><td>Discard</td><td>Discard</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>Papers and photos:</strong> if you can't deal with them right away, put them in sealed bags in the freezer. Freezing stops mold and gives you time to find a document recovery service. The National Archives publishes guidance on saving family papers and photographs.</p>

  <h2>Irreplaceable and high-value items</h2>
  <p>Restoration contents departments are good at volume. Fine art, antiques, heirlooms, musical instruments and quilts may need a <strong>specialist conservator</strong>. You can ask for those items to be handled separately and ask your adjuster whether specialist restoration will be covered. It often costs less than replacement, which makes it easier to approve.</p>

  <h2>How contents are paid</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Actual cash value (ACV) first:</strong> many policies pay replacement cost minus depreciation for age and wear.</li>
    <li><strong>Recoverable depreciation:</strong> with replacement cost coverage, the depreciation is usually paid once you replace the item, within a deadline set by the policy. Keep every receipt.</li>
    <li><strong>"Like kind and quality":</strong> insurers price replacements with comparable items. If the comparison item is lower quality than what you owned, show them listings for genuinely equivalent items.</li>
    <li><strong>Special limits:</strong> jewelry, firearms, collectibles, cash and some electronics often have lower limits unless separately scheduled.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The pack-back</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Check items against the inventory</strong> as they come back, before signing a delivery receipt.</li>
    <li><strong>Open boxes of fragile items</strong> while the crew is still there.</li>
    <li><strong>Note any odor</strong> on textiles or furniture that went out for smoke cleaning.</li>
    <li><strong>Report damage or missing items in writing</strong> right away, to the company and your adjuster.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Before the next time: a home inventory</h2>
  <p>A ten-minute video walk-through of each room, opening closets and drawers, saved somewhere other than your house, makes any future contents claim far easier. Narrate as you go: what each item is, roughly when you bought it and what it cost.</p>

